  },
  {
   "id": "c-a10-recoil",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "a-10"
   ],
   "text": "The GAU-8 generates roughly 10,000 pounds of recoil, more than a single A-10 engine's thrust."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-a10-gear",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "a-10"
   ],
   "text": "The nose gear is offset to keep the firing barrel on the aircraft centerline."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-f15-response",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "f-15",
    "mig-25"
   ],
   "text": "The F-15 program was shaped by the US reaction to the MiG-25, whose capabilities were overestimated."
  },
  {
   "id": "src-bell-x-1-vcl",
   "type": "source",
   "title": "EC VCL: Bell X-1 (Jul 2026)",
   "kind": "reference"
  },
  {
   "id": "ev-bell-x-1-1",
   "type": "event",
   "eventType": "decision",
   "date": {
    "year": 1945,
    "month": 3
   },
   "subjectId": "bell-x-1",
   "label": "Bell contracted to build three XS-1 supersonic research aircraft"
  },
  {
   "id": "ev-bell-x-1-3",
   "type": "event",
   "eventType": "record",
   "date": {
    "year": 1947,
    "month": 10
   },
   "subjectId": "bell-x-1",
   "label": "First supersonic flight, Mach 1.06, Yeager, Glamorous Glennis"
  },
  {
   "id": "ev-bell-x-1-4",
   "type": "event",
   "eventType": "retirement",
   "date": {
    "year": 1950,
    "month": 5
   },
   "subjectId": "bell-x-1",
   "label": "X-1-1 retired to the National Air Museum after 82 airframe flights"
  },
  {
   "id": "chuck-yeager",
   "type": "person",
   "name": "Chuck Yeager",
   "note": "Flew the first supersonic flight; chosen as the most experienced USAF test pilot; flew the record flight with two broken ribs."
  },
  {
   "id": "jack-ridley",
   "type": "person",
   "name": "Jack Ridley",
   "note": "Flight engineer; cut the broom handle that let Yeager seal the hatch with broken ribs."
  },
  {
   "id": "dick-frost",
   "type": "person",
   "name": "Dick Frost",
   "note": "Proposed the worm-gear drive that let the pilot adjust the whole horizontal stabilizer in flight."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-01",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "On 14 October 1947 the Bell X-1, piloted by Chuck Yeager, became the first airplane to exceed the speed of sound at Mach 1.06.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-02",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The X-1 was air-launched from a B-29 and powered by a four-chamber Reaction Motors XLR11 rocket of 6,000 lbf burning liquid oxygen and ethyl alcohol.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM; chuckyeager.org; San Diego Air & Space Museum"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-03",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The fuselage was modeled on the .50-caliber machine-gun bullet, a shape known to be stable at supersonic speed.",
   "sourceNote": "San Diego Air & Space Museum"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-04",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The X-1 used extremely thin, strong wings (10 percent wing, 8 percent tail) to delay shock formation.",
   "sourceNote": "chuckyeager.org; National Interest"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-05",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "Bell was contracted on 16 March 1945 to build three XS-1 research aircraft, establishing the pure research aircraft concept.",
   "sourceNote": "San Diego Air & Space Museum"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-06",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "In early October 1947 Yeager lost elevator effectiveness at about Mach 0.92 due to shock-induced flow separation at the tail.",
   "sourceNote": "SPS Aviation"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-07",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "Dick Frost proposed the worm-gear arrangement letting the pilot drive the whole stabilizer; Yeager regained control with about two degrees of stabilizer on the following flight.",
   "sourceNote": "SPS Aviation"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-08",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "On the record flight the upgraded adjustable stabilizer let Yeager make fine angle changes that maintained pitch authority approaching Mach 1.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-09",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "Tilting the entire stabilizer restores pitch authority because the full surface includes flow ahead of the shock, unlike the hinged elevator sitting in separated air.",
   "sourceNote": "National Interest; Smithsonian accounts; standard aerodynamics"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-10",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The in-flight-adjustable stabilizer idea was proposed by NACA during the P-38 compressibility investigation and shelved; the concept became a defining control feature of the X-1.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian Air & Space (EC VCL, P-38 package claim 8)"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-11",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The movable-tail concept became standard on the supersonic fighters that followed, including the F-100, F-104, F-4, F-15, and F-16.",
   "sourceNote": "National Interest"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-12",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "Days before the flight Yeager broke two ribs falling from a horse, concealed it, and sealed the hatch using a broom handle cut by Jack Ridley.",
   "sourceNote": "Yeager autobiography; fr.Wikipedia corroborates the fall"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-13",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "Yeager, a WWII ace with 13 victories, named the aircraft Glamorous Glennis for his wife.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-14",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "Yeager described the real barrier as not in the sky but in knowledge and experience of supersonic flight.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-15",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The X-1 program flew 78 program flights, later reaching Mach 1.45 and 71,900 feet.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-16",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "conflicting",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The altitude of the Mach 1.06 record run is reported as 42,000 feet by the Smithsonian, 43,000 feet by multiple sources, and 45,000 feet by chuckyeager.org.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM; AVI-8; SPS Aviation; chuckyeager.org"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-17",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "conflicting",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The record speed in mph is reported as about 700 mph by the Smithsonian and 807 mph by sources using different airspeed conversions.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM; SPS Aviation"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-18",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "conflicting",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The drop altitude from the B-29 is reported variously as 20,000, 21,000, and 23,000 to 25,000 feet.",
   "sourceNote": "Smithsonian NASM; chuckyeager.org; SPS Aviation"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-19",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "verified",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The flight and its data were classified following the achievement.",
   "sourceNote": "SPS Aviation; Smithsonian context"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-bell-x-1-20",
   "type": "claim",
   "status": "conflicting",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
   "text": "The timing of Yeager's rib injury is reported as two days before the flight by most accounts and the day before by fr.Wikipedia.",
   "sourceNote": "Yeager autobiography accounts; fr.Wikipedia"
  },
  {
   "id": "story-bell-x-1",
   "type": "story",
   "title": "The Shelved Fix That Broke the Sound Barrier",
   "hook": "THE X-1 BROKE THE SOUND BARRIER IN 1947 WITH A FIX THAT WAS NOT INVENTED FOR IT. IT HAD BEEN ON A SHELF SINCE THE P-38 INVESTIGATION (1943).",
   "aboutIds": [
    "bell-x-1"
   ],
